# InterGenOS — explicit-enable list.
# Services we deliberately want active by default on installed systems.
# Add new lines here when you want a service auto-enabled on first boot;
# add nothing here for "user installs it, user enables it" semantics.
#
# Already covered by other preset files:
#   - gdm.service          (90-gdm.preset)
#   - nftables.service     (90-nftables.preset)
#   - systemd-* services   (90-systemd.preset, upstream)

enable NetworkManager.service
enable apparmor.service
enable systemd-oomd.service

# bluetooth.service — enabled by default to match desktop-distro convention.
# Survey (2026-06-10, GBC003 G3-9): Fedora, Debian, Ubuntu and openSUSE all ship
# bluez with `enable bluetooth.service` in their vendor preset (status reads
# "vendor preset: enabled"); Arch is the lone outlier, leaving it disabled under
# its minimal-defaults philosophy (the same deny-by-default stance our whitelist
# otherwise follows). InterGenOS ships GNOME as a desktop, and a fresh user
# expects BT mice/keyboards/headphones to just work — without this line the
# install-time `systemctl preset-all` reverts the unit via the 99- `disable *`
# catch-all (observed on a development machine: "Removed bluetooth.target.wants/bluetooth.service")
# leaving bluetoothd inert and GNOME's Bluetooth toggle non-functional.
# Security note: enabling the unit starts bluetoothd but does NOT make the
# adapter discoverable/pairable — GNOME keeps the adapter non-discoverable until
# the user opens Settings, so the surface is the local daemon, not an open
# pairing window (the standard desktop posture). bluez ships in the desktop tier
# (always present with the required GNOME stack); the line is a no-op if absent.
enable bluetooth.service

# (The G3-3 intergenos-greeter-background.service was retired in GBC003.1 —
# its Before=gdm ordering edge caused the AMD first-boot GDM crash (PI-14), and
# the greeter background is now set in the gnome-shell theme CSS, not via a
# boot-time selector. No whitelist line is needed.)

# pkm-check-updates.timer is the SOLE producer of
# /var/lib/pkm/available-updates.json, which the default-enabled
# pkm-notifier@intergenos.org GNOME extension (and the motd-pkm-pending hook)
# read to surface available (security) updates. pkm/build.sh enables the timer
# at image-build time, but the install-time `systemctl preset-all` (users.py)
# reverts it via the 99- `disable *` catch-all unless it is whitelisted here --
# so without this line the update-notifier ships enabled but permanently inert
# (its JSON is never written, and a timer cannot be D-Bus-activated as a
# fallback). Update awareness is security-relevant. (GBC002.5, 2026-06-08.)
enable pkm-check-updates.timer

# logrotate.timer rotates the system logs, including
# /etc/logrotate.d/intergen-tool-dispatch — the snippet the tree has shipped
# since InterGen's tool-dispatch audit log landed, with nothing consuming it
# until the logrotate package existed. Without this line the install-time
# `systemctl preset-all` reverts the timer through the 99- `disable *`
# catch-all, so the rotation would ship present and permanently inert: exactly
# the failure recorded for pkm-check-updates.timer above. An audit log that
# grows without bound is a disk-exhaustion path on a long-lived install, and a
# log nothing rotates is also a log nobody can keep.
enable logrotate.timer

# switcheroo-control.service is the D-Bus service GNOME Shell asks which GPU an
# application should run on; it is what puts "Launch using Discrete Graphics
# Card" in the application menu on hybrid-graphics machines. Its unit is
# Type=dbus with WantedBy=graphical.target, so without a whitelist line the
# 99- `disable *` catch-all leaves it installed and never started, and the menu
# entry is silently absent with no error anywhere to explain why.
enable switcheroo-control.service

# Explicit disables — override the upstream 90-systemd.preset, which `enable`s
# systemd-networkd. Preset resolution is first-match-wins across lexically-
# sorted files, so these 80- lines beat 90-systemd.preset's enable (and are
# reached before the 99- `disable *` catch-all). NetworkManager (above) owns
# networking on InterGenOS; systemd-networkd is redundant, and its
# wait-online unit delays network-online.target (~6s observed on the GBC001.4
# boot), which gates GDM. NM provides DHCP via its own backend.
# networkmanager/build.sh disables networkd at package-build time, but a later
# `systemctl preset-all` (build-squashfs customize hook) re-enabled it from
# 90-systemd.preset — these lines close that loop durably. (GBC001.5,
# 2026-06-05.)
disable systemd-networkd.service
disable systemd-networkd.socket
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service

# forge-tui.service has ConditionKernelCommandLine=igos.mode=install-tui
# so it only fires in install-tui mode. In live + install-gui modes the
# condition is not met and the unit is a no-op. Enabling here so it's
# in multi-user.target.wants/ for install-tui boots to find.
enable forge-tui.service
